  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nCold advection continues behind an exiting cold front. A band \nof showers in the vicinity of a secondary trough will continue \nto shift south before ending by early morning. There could be a\nfew showers with lake enhancement early this morning, though \ncurrent satellite imagery and model soundings indicate moisture\nshould become shallow by morning, with little support for \nprecip. \n\nClearing should occur through the afternoon under building high\npressure. The high is expected to be overhead tonight, with \nclear skies and light wind. Efficient radiational cooling \nshould result in below freezing temperatures for most of the \narea. Nudged guidance toward the colder solutions with these \nfavorable conditions. Upgraded the Freeze Watch to a Freeze \nWarning for the area tonight into Sunday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate a closed low should remain off of the\nMid Atlantic/Carolina coast through much of the upcoming week,\nwith a trough along the western CONUS. A ridge is progged to\nbuild in between these two systems across the Central CONUS to \nthe Upper Ohio Valley/Great Lakes region, and persist through\nthe week. Dry weather is expected through this period, with\ngradually warming temperatures through mid week as 500 mb\nheights rise. Readings by mid to late week should be around 5\ndegrees above average.",
